cover=dict(
 headline='EssilorLuxottica: a defensive core, and a bet on smart glasses',
 sub1=DATE+' · Syz Research',
 notice='Prices are as of the close on 27 November 2026. Company figures come from the 2025 annual results, the first-half 2026 results and the third-quarter revenue release of 20 October 2026. Multiples use Bloomberg consensus earnings. This note is not a recommendation for any individual investor.',
 kpis=[('Share price','EUR 172.15','−36% since the start of 2026','neg'),
       ('Forward P/E','22.1x','Ten-year average: 27.5x',None),
       ('Q3 2026 revenue growth','+7.2%','At constant exchange rates',None),
       ('AI glasses sold in 2025','Above 7m','Company figure',None)],
 h2='A steady business, with a fast-growing and controversial new line',
 left=[('sub2','The market the group works in'),
  ('p','EssilorLuxottica is the world\'s largest eyewear company, with revenue of EUR 28.5bn in 2025. It makes lenses (Essilor, Varilux, Transitions, Stellest), designs and distributes frames and sunglasses (Ray-Ban and Oakley, which it owns, and licensed brands such as Prada, Chanel and Armani) and sells to the public through its own stores and online (Sunglass Hut, LensCrafters, GrandVision).'),
  ('p','Demand is steady and largely non-discretionary. Glasses are a medical need for a large share of the world\'s population, populations are ageing and myopia among children is rising fast: a widely cited projection is that about half of the world\'s population could be short-sighted by 2050. Few competitors can match the group\'s scale, which is why the shares have long been seen as a defensive holding.'),
  ('p','Three growth areas sit on top of this base. Myopia management lenses such as Stellest grew 24% in the third quarter. Hearing glasses (Nuance Audio) take the group into hearing care, where more than a billion people live with some hearing loss. And AI glasses, built with Meta, are the largest and the most debated of the three.')],
 right=[('sub2','Why the share price has fallen so far'),
  ('p','The shares closed at EUR 172.15, 36% below their level at the start of 2026 and 46% below the record close of EUR 320.7 in November 2025. Expected earnings per share for the next twelve months barely moved over the year, from EUR 7.87 to EUR 7.80. The whole fall comes from the multiple, which went from about 40 times earnings to 22 times.'),
  ('p','The market appears to have stopped paying a premium for the smart-glasses story. The ramp-up weighed on margins (the adjusted operating margin fell 70 basis points to 16.0% in 2025, with US tariffs also a factor), competition from larger technology groups became easier to imagine, and privacy concerns grew.'),
  ('p','The third-quarter release of 20 October was the turning point: sales rose 7.2% at constant exchange rates to EUR 7,486m, above analysts\' expectations, and the shares rose 15.5% the next day, from EUR 150.5 to EUR 173.8.'),
  ('sub2','What this means for investors'),
  ('p','The lens, frame and retail business gives the group a stable base, and the shares now trade on about 22 times expected earnings against a ten-year average of 27.5 times. How much the market will pay for the smart-glasses story is the open question, and it depends on adoption, competition and public acceptance.')])
meta=dict(
 title='Meta glasses: the engine, and the privacy question',
 sub='The group\'s largest growth line is also the one most exposed to public opinion and regulation',
 kpis=[('AI glasses sold in 2025','Above 7m','Company figure'),
       ('Capacity target','10m a year','By end of 2026'),
       ('Meta\'s stake','Just under 3%','Reported, bought in 2025'),
       ('Wearables, Q3 2026','+20%','Revenue growth')],
 left=[('h3','What the Meta glasses are'),
  ('p','Ray-Ban Meta (since September 2023) and Oakley Meta (since June 2025) look like ordinary glasses but contain a camera, microphones, speakers and the Meta AI assistant. The wearer can take photos and videos, make calls, play music and ask the assistant about what they see. EssilorLuxottica designs, makes and sells the frames; Meta provides the technology, the software and the AI.'),
  ('p','At Meta\'s Connect event in late September the companies added a camera-free model, Ray-Ban Meta Audio (from USD 349), a third generation of Ray-Ban Meta and new styles. Meta\'s AI agent, Muse, is due on the glasses, first in the United States.'),
  ('h3','Why they matter for the group'),
  ('p','**Size.** More than 7 million pairs sold in 2025 made smart glasses the first new eyewear category in decades to reach that scale so fast. Press reports put wearables at close to 6.5% of third-quarter revenue; the company gives the growth rate (+20%) but not the amount.'),
  ('p','**Reach.** People wear glasses all day, so they could become the everyday device for AI assistants, as phones were for apps. The group brings what technology companies lack: frame design, prescription lenses, brands and a global store network.'),
  ('p','**Position.** Industry trackers put Meta\'s share of smart-glasses shipments between about 70% and 85%, depending on the definition, and EssilorLuxottica is its eyewear partner. Google, Samsung and Apple are reported to be investing in their own AI glasses, so the lead could be tested.'),
  ('p','**Dependence.** The success is shared with a partner that is also a shareholder, and a growing part of the group\'s growth now depends on one partner\'s roadmap.')],
 right=[('h3','The privacy question'),
  ('p','Camera glasses raise a problem that phones do not: the person being filmed may not know.'),
  ('p','**Recording.** Meta says an LED shows when the camera is recording. A German test found the light too faint to see in daylight, and reports say it does not come on when the camera-based AI features are used, so people nearby may have no signal.'),
  ('p','**Data.** Photos, voice and what the camera sees can be processed by Meta\'s AI. Regulators ask where the images go and how long they are kept. In Europe, images of identifiable people are personal data.'),
  ('p','**Faces.** Meta has not launched a consumer facial-recognition feature, but press reports say it has considered one for future models, and civil-liberty groups oppose the idea. Researchers have shown that footage from the glasses can be passed through outside tools to identify strangers.'),
  ('p','**Reactions in Europe.** In early October the Dutch chain Hans Anders stopped selling the glasses in the Netherlands and Belgium, and Wehkamp paused sales. The Dutch data protection authority warned against sharing videos of identifiable people without permission, a consumer group threatened legal action and a German organisation filed a criminal complaint. Calls for bans have been heard in several countries, but reports say the Dutch government is not rushing to one.'),
  ('h3','Why it matters for the investment case'),
  ('p','The data is Meta\'s, but the brand on the frame is Ray-Ban\'s, so the reputational risk reaches the group. A retailer pulling the product shows how fast sentiment can turn, and new rules could force design changes that add cost. If wearing camera glasses becomes socially awkward, as with Google Glass a decade ago, adoption could slow. Camera-free models show the group can sell connected glasses without the issue.')],
 concl='Meta glasses help explain the 40 times earnings the shares were valued at a year ago, and part of the fall since. The category is growing fast and the group is its main manufacturer and distributor, but the line carries three exposures: dependence on one partner, competition from larger technology groups, and public and regulatory acceptance. At roughly 6.5% of sales the group could absorb a setback; a valuation built on their growth could be more sensitive.')
fiche=dict(
 title='EssilorLuxottica (EL FP)',
 subtitle='Stable earnings, a lower multiple and a smart-glasses debate that is still open',
 kpis=[('Share price','EUR 172.15','−36% year to date','neg'),
       ('Forward P/E','22.1x','Ten-year average: 27.5x',None),
       ('Expected EPS (12m)','EUR 7.80','EUR 7.87 a year ago',None),
       ('Q3 2026 revenue growth','+7.2%','Constant exchange rates',None)],
 tech=[('Technical trend','Rebounding'),('Support','EUR 150 / 139'),('Resistance','EUR 176 / 194'),('200-day average','EUR 176.4')],
 techline='The shares stand just below their 200-day average (EUR 176.4) and above the 40-day average (EUR 170.9). A close above EUR 194, the 6 November high, could confirm a change of trend; EUR 150, the level before the results, and EUR 139, the September low, are the levels to watch on the downside.',
 left=[('h3','1. What the company does'),
  ('p','EssilorLuxottica was formed by the 2018 merger of Essilor (lenses) and Luxottica (frames and retail), and added GrandVision\'s stores in 2021. Revenue in 2025 was EUR 28.5bn, up 7.5% (11.2% at constant exchange rates). It sells lenses and frames to opticians and, increasingly, directly to consumers in its own stores and online. Wearables, myopia management and hearing glasses are the growth areas.'),
  ('h3','2. Why it could do well'),
  ('p','Third-quarter revenue rose 7.2% at constant exchange rates, with Asia-Pacific up 15.7%, EMEA up 7.5% and North America up 4.7%. In the first half, revenue grew 9.7% and adjusted operating profit 15%, at a margin of 18.6%. Myopia management grew 24% in the third quarter and wearables 20%, with the products shown in September still to contribute. Much of the business recurs: people replace lenses and frames regularly and need them for medical reasons.'),
  ('h3','3. What sets it apart'),
  ('p','No competitor combines lenses, frames, brands and retail at this scale. Ray-Ban and Oakley are among the best-known eyewear brands. The medtech side includes Stellest for myopia, Nuance Audio for hearing (a second generation launched this autumn) and, with Meta, the largest position in AI glasses.')],
 right=[('h3','4. Is the share cheap?'),
  ('p','Cheaper than it has been for most of the past decade. The shares trade on 22.1 times expected earnings, against 27.5 over ten years and about 40 a year ago, while the earnings forecast has hardly changed (EUR 7.80 against EUR 7.87). The forward multiple touched 17.7x on 18 September, its lowest in ten years. A lower multiple is not the same as cheap: part of the earlier premium paid for smart-glasses growth, and the ramp-up has diluted margins.'),
  ('h3','5. Main risks'),
  ('p','Dependence on Meta and its product roadmap. Competition from Google, Samsung, Apple and Chinese makers as the market grows. Privacy rules and retailer reactions in Europe. Margin dilution while smart glasses scale. US tariffs and currency moves, with North America at about 43% of third-quarter sales. A weaker consumer for licensed and sun brands.'),
  ('h3','6. What to watch next'),
  ('p','Full-year 2026 results, due early in 2027, with the wearables margin; holiday sales of the new Ray-Ban Meta models and the rollout of Muse on the glasses; regulatory steps on camera glasses in Europe and whether more retailers follow Hans Anders; products from Google, Samsung and Apple; any change in Meta\'s stake or in the partnership.')],
 conclusion='EssilorLuxottica combines a stable lens, frame and retail business with a fast-growing but contested smart-glasses line. Over the past year its earnings forecasts have hardly moved while its multiple has fallen from about 40 to 22 times, which shows how much of the share price rested on sentiment around Meta glasses. The outcome could depend on adoption, on competition and, more and more, on the social and regulatory acceptance of camera glasses.')
